  "worked_solution": "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\u2032}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4\\alpha {e}^{4x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{4x}) & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ (12-6)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}.\n$$",
